    {
      "paperId": "10.2514/6.2026-3069",
      "doi": "10.2514/6.2026-3069",
      "title": "Applying Program Theory Innovation Model for ECLSS Development for India?s Gaganyaan Human Space Flight Program",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Sundararajan, Venkatesan"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "The Indian Space Research Organization (ISRO) implements the national space activities under the policy direction of the Space Commission constituted within the Department of Space (DoS), Government of India that directly reports to the Prime Minister of India. The stated vision of ISRO is to ?Harness, sustain and augment space technology for national development, while pursuing space science research and planetary exploration.? Over the past five decades, ISRO has established India as a leading spacefaring nation through the Indian Remote Sensing (IRS) satellites program for civilian earth observation and disaster management, the Indian National Satellites (INSAT) program for telecommunications and broadband network, Navigation with Indian Constellation (NavIC), and through dedicated planetary and astronomy space science missions. In 2018, the Indian government committed the nation to pursuing human spaceflight with a budget allocation of ?10,000 Crores (~ $1.4 billion) and subsequently provide a net additional funding of ?11,170 Crore in 2024, with the objective to spur R&D in the nation and enable India to participate in future global space exploration endeavors for national benefits. In January 2019, ISRO established the Human Space Flight Center (HSFC) and is currently embarking on an ambitious ?Gaganyaan? program to realize human spaceflight capabilities within the nation, to undertake a crewed mission by 2027-28 to carry a crew of up to three to Low Earth Orbit (LEO) for up to three days and return them safely to Indian sea waters and from 2028 to begin missions to establish the Indian Space Station, ?Bharatiya Antariksh Station? (BAS), in a circular Low Earth Orbit (LEO) of about 400-450 km by 2035. The Environmental Control and Life Support Systems (ECLSS) that provides a safe and habitable environment for Crew Health & Performance (CHP) in the harsh conditions of space is the most critical component for any human spaceflight (HSF) mission. The development of ECLSS is a strategic capability in the space domain that only a few countries have achieved. In this paper, I provide the stakeholder & technical requirements and the structure of the major ECLSS subsystems through MBSE SysML diagrams. Given the significance and novel nature of ECLSS development for India?s emerging human spaceflight program, I then applied the innovation model from program theory to develop and understand the outcomes chain for the ECLSS Innovation.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "openAccess_pdf": {},
